<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/docs/getting-started' }, { label: 'Identity & OAuth' }, { label: 'Coder & the Control Plane' }]}>
  <h1>Coder &amp; the Control Plane</h1>

  <p>
    For the overall identity model, see <a href="/docs/getting-started/token-guide">Identity & OAuth</a>.
    This page looks at the Coder side of that model: why the control plane holds the identity and
    credentials, and how the orchestrator workspace fits in.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="1" id="the-split">The Coder / OpenFlows split</StepHeading>
  <p>
    One idea explains the whole architecture:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Coder governs <em>where</em> agents run. OpenFlows governs <em>how</em> agents
    coordinate.</strong>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Coder is the execution substrate. It provides real identities and login, disposable workspaces, a
    central place where model calls happen, and the network and admin controls that keep things safe.
    OpenFlows is the layer on top that turns that pile of workspaces into a team - it plans, reviews,
    recovers, and merges.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="2" id="why-this-is-secure">Why this keeps identity safe</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Most of OpenFlows' security comes directly from this division:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>AI calls happen only in the control plane</strong> through a central gateway, which means worker workspaces never hold a model key.</li>
    <li><strong>Git identity is a scoped OAuth link</strong>, so no shared raw token ever sits in a workspace.</li>
    <li><strong>Worker workspaces are thin and disposable</strong> - no framework, no keys, tight network - because the intelligence and credentials they would need live centrally, not inside them.</li>
  </ul>

  <StepHeading step="3" id="where-trust-lives">Where trust lives</StepHeading>
  <p>
    There is one long-lived, trusted component - the <strong>orchestrator workspace</strong> - which
    legitimately holds the credentials and runs the coordination loop. It is the "brain" that hosts the
    coordination and is the one place that holds the Coder and GitHub access the workers never see.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything else is ephemeral and untrusted. The asymmetry is deliberate: the trusted, long-lived
    component does the coordination; the untrusted, disposable components do the work. Trust is
    concentrated in a single auditable place rather than spread across many long-lived pieces.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Nobody is anonymous">
    Every action an agent takes inherits a real identity through Coder's login and the scoped OAuth
    link. There are no shared, mysterious accounts. When something is merged or commented on, it is
    attributable to the tenant's identity, and the trail is auditable.
  </Callout>

  <Callout type="info" title="Continue reading">
    See <a href="/docs/getting-started/token-guide-security">Security best practices</a> for how the
    identity model holds up in practice, and
    <a href="/docs/concepts/coder-integration">Coder Integration</a> for the runtime contract.
  </Callout>
